{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"I submitted the mitigation, and the endpoint returned `Submission received` for the mitigation stage.\n\n**Root cause:** The `rate` deployment had `RATE_BACKEND_QPS_LIMIT=20`, which is far too low for normal traffic. Requests queued behind the limiter and then hit deadlines or `ResourceExhausted`. Search's retry policy (3 attempts, 750 ms timeout) added more load on top.\n\n**Fix applied:**\n- Raised `RATE_BACKEND_QPS_LIMIT` from `20` to `1000` on the `rate` deployment, which rolled out cleanly.\n- Restarted `search` so it dropped stale connections to the old rate pod. Its `Unavailable` errors stopped after the restart.\n\n**Checks before submitting:** All 19 pods are running. Search and rate logs show no warnings in the last 30–60 seconds, and a frontend `/hotels` request returned HTTP 200.\n\n**Not verified:** I didn't confirm end-to-end latency under load, and the `/hotels` response came back with an empty feature list. I didn't investigate whether that's expected for the test coordinates.\n\n**Other notes:**\n- `rate` and `search` crashed once each at startup because MongoDB and Consul weren't ready yet. Both recovered on their own and I didn't change anything for that.\n- I didn't have access to the rate service source, so the new QPS value of 1000 is my choice rather than a documented default."}}
